Charlie Munger, the late vice chairman of Berkshire Hathaway, cultivated an investment
philosophy that combined rigorous financial analysis with insights from psychology,
mathematics, and economics. This report synthesizes Mungerʼs core principles, mathematical
frameworks, and historical performance data to provide a blueprint for institutional investors
seeking to emulate his success.
Mungerʼs approach centered on concentrated value investing through a latticework of mental
models. His strategy generated 19.8% annualized returns from 19621975 in his partnership and
delivered 13% CAGR at Berkshire Hathaway from 19922022 . Key pillars included:
Buying high-quality businesses at fair prices rather than mediocre companies at discounts
Applying multidisciplinary analysis to assess competitive moats and management quality
Maintaining extreme portfolio concentration 35 positions typically comprising 75% of
assets )
Leveraging compound growth through multi-decade holding periods
Munger shifted traditional Graham-style value investing by emphasizing business quality over
statistical cheapness:
Traditional Value vs. Mungerʼs Approach
Focus on low P/E and P/B ratios Focus on durable competitive advantages
Mean reversion expectations Growth in intrinsic value over time
Diversification 40+ holdings) Extreme concentration 35 stocks)
Margin of safety through price Margin of safety through quality
This philosophy is exemplified by Berkshireʼs 1972 acquisition of Seeʼs Candies at 3x book value
– a premium price for a business generating 25% pre-tax margins .
